## What is the difference between a `String` and a `StringBuilder`?

1. `String` is immutable: once created, a string cannot be changed. So, if we `String str = "Some text"`, then we cannot change `str` later e.g. by writing `str = "Some other text"`.
2. `String` is not thread-safe: when multiple threads are using `String`, it can be a problem. If multiple threads are using the `String` at the same time, the same value can be returned for different threads (e.g. `String` might be changed by the first thread, then another thread might read the same value).
For more details, see [this thread](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1534678/whats-the-difference-between-string-and-stringbuilder).

### Why is it better to use `StringBuie that, if you use `String` in the same scope, you would not have a conflict with multiple threads.

