# Explanation

> Explanation:
> - The attack uses semicolons to separate the alias from the query
> - The attack uses the semicolon as an operator to separate the alias from the query

# How it works

> How it works:
> - The first step in injecting the code is to block access to the query string
> - In this instance, semicolons are blocked via regex
> - Next, an attacker uses semicolons to separate the alias and the query
> - This injects query manipulation logic to the alias
> - Then, the attacker injects code alongside the alias

# Mitigation

> Mitigation:
> - In order to mitigate this attack, the query-building can be separated from the alias
> - This will allow the alias to be used alone
> - The logic can be performed after the alias is built
> - The attacker will then be unable to use SQL keywords or logic from the alias in the query

# Resources

> Resources:
> - [OWASP Top 10 - A6: Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/OWASP_Top_Ten_2017/Top_10-2017_A6-Security_Misconfiguration)

