# Thread methods
In previous section, we have already seen how to create and use threads in Java. However, there are a few more methods that can be used to manage threads.

## Running thread
There are two ways to start a thread. First one is to use `Thread.start()` method. This method must be called from within the thread itself, otherwise you will get `IllegalThreadStateException`.

```java
package com.example.startthread;

public class StartThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread is running");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StartThread startThread = new StartThread();
        startThread.start();
    }
}
```

In output you will see something like this:

```
Thread is running
```

Second, you can use the `start()` method also from another thread. The thread will start after the method returns. In the example above, you can see that the main thread will start the thread before it's finished.

```java
package com.example.startthread;

public class StartThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread is running");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StartThread startThread = new StartThread();
        startThread.start();
        System.out.println("Main thread is finished");
    }
}
```

In output you will see something like this:

```
Main thread is finished
Thread is running
```

When main thread starts the thread, it prints out `Main thread is finished`, then after it is finished, the `start()` method returns and the thread continues its `run()` method.


## Joining thread
In many cases, threads can be started and stopped. To make sure that the current thread will not terminate until the thread you're waiting for is finished, you must use the `join()` method.

